# Code Quality

## Functions

- Single responsibility: a function does ONE thing.
- Use guard clauses (early returns) instead of deeply nested if/else.
- Extract complex conditions into named variables or functions.

```typescript
// ❌ Avoid
function process(user) {
  if (user) {
    if (user.isActive) {
      if (user.role === "admin") {
        // actual logic buried here
      }
    }
  }
}

// ✅ Prefer
function process(user: User) {
  if (!user) return;
  if (!user.isActive) return;
  if (user.role !== "admin") return;
  // actual logic at the top level
}
```

## Naming

- Never use single-letter variables outside of short loop indices.
- No magic numbers or strings — extract into named constants.

```typescript
// ❌ Avoid
if (retries > 3) { ... }

// ✅ Prefer
const MAX_RETRY_COUNT = 3;
if (retries > MAX_RETRY_COUNT) { ... }
```

## Variables

- Always use `const`. Use `let` only when reassignment is needed. Never use `var`.
- Never mutate state or function arguments — always return a new reference.

## Error Handling

- Always use try/catch with meaningful, contextual error messages.
- Never silence errors with an empty catch block.

```typescript
// ❌ Avoid
try { ... } catch {}

// ✅ Prefer
try {
  const data = await fetchOrder(id);
  return data;
} catch (error) {
  console.error(`[fetchOrder] Failed for id=${id}:`, error);
  throw new Error('Could not load order. Please try again.');
}
```

## Comments

- Comment the WHY, not the WHAT.
- TODOs must include context: `// TODO: remove after API v2 migration — @yourname`

## What to always flag in generated code

1. Duplicated logic → suggest extraction
2. Unhandled edge cases (null, empty, error)
3. Magic numbers/strings
4. Missing types on function signatures
5. Prop drilling beyond 2 levels
